1
00:05:43,846 --> 00:05:49,846
An epic drama of adventure and exploration.

2
00:11:27,692 --> 00:11:33,692
2001: A Space Odyssey is a science fiction, mystery,
adventure film directed by Stanley Kubrick.

3
00:17:11,538 --> 00:17:17,538
Humanity finds a mysterious object buried beneath the lunar surface and sets off to find its
origins with the help of HAL 9000, the world's most advanced super computer.

4
00:22:55,384 --> 00:23:01,384
Dr. David Bowman, played by Keir Dullea.

5
00:28:39,230 --> 00:28:45,230
Dr. Frank Poole, played by Gary Lockwood.

6
00:34:23,076 --> 00:34:29,076
Dr. Heywood Floyd, played by William Sylvester.

7
00:40:06,923 --> 00:40:12,923
HAL 9000 (voice), played by Douglas Rain.

8
00:45:50,769 --> 00:45:56,769
Moonwatcher, played by Daniel Richter.

9
00:51:34,615 --> 00:51:40,615
Dr. Andrei Smyslov, played by Leonard Rossiter.

10
00:57:18,461 --> 00:57:24,461
2001: A Space Odyssey is a 1968 epic science fiction film produced and directed
by Stanley Kubrick, who co-wrote the screenplay with Arthur C.

11
01:03:02,307 --> 01:03:08,307
Its plot was inspired by several short stories optioned from Clarke,
primarily "The Sentinel" (1951) and "Encounter in the Dawn" (1953).

12
01:08:46,153 --> 01:08:52,153
The film stars Keir Dullea, Gary Lockwood, William Sylvester, and Douglas Rain, and follows a voyage by astronauts,
scientists, and the sentient supercomputer HAL 9000 to Jupiter to investigate an alien monolith.

13
01:14:30,000 --> 01:14:36,000
The film is noted for its scientifically accurate depiction of
spaceflight, pioneering special effects, and ambiguous themes.

14
01:20:13,846 --> 01:20:19,846
Kubrick avoided conventional cinematic and narrative techniques; dialogue is
used sparingly, and long sequences are accompanied only by music.

15
01:25:57,692 --> 01:26:03,692
Shunning the convention that major film productions should feature original music, 2001: A Space Odyssey takes for its soundtrack numerous
works of classical music, including pieces by Richard Strauss, Johann Strauss II, Aram Khachaturian, and György Ligeti.

16
01:31:41,538 --> 01:31:47,538
Polarising critics after its release, 2001: A Space Odyssey has since been subject to a variety of
interpretations, ranging from the darkly apocalyptic to an optimistic reappraisal of the hopes of humanity.

17
01:37:25,384 --> 01:37:31,384
Critics noted its exploration of themes such as human evolution, technology,
artificial intelligence, and the possibility of extraterrestrial life.

18
01:43:09,230 --> 01:43:15,230
It was nominated for four Academy Awards, winning Kubrick the award for his direction of
the visual effects, the only Academy Award the director would receive.

19
01:48:53,076 --> 01:48:59,076
The film is widely regarded as one of the greatest
and most influential films ever made.

20
01:54:36,923 --> 01:54:42,923
In 1991, it was selected by the United States Library of Congress
for preservation in the National Film Registry.

21
02:00:20,769 --> 02:00:26,769
In 2022, 2001: A Space Odyssey placed in the top ten of Sight & Sound's
decennial critics' poll, and topped their directors' poll.

22
02:06:04,615 --> 02:06:10,615
A sequel, 2010: The Year We Make Contact, was released in 1984; it was based on
Clarke's novel 2010: Odyssey Two and made without Kubrick's involvement.

23
02:11:48,461 --> 02:11:54,461
Clarke published a novelisation of 2001 (in part written concurrently with the screenplay) soon
after the film's 1968 release, for which Kubrick received co-writing credit.

24
02:17:32,307 --> 02:17:38,307
The film explores themes of man vs machine, moon, jupiter,
artificial intelligence (a.i.), based on novel or book.

25
02:23:16,153 --> 02:23:22,153
The tone is thought_provoking and mind_bending.
